  "adjudication": "P1's per-language emit cells are computed from src/ast/real-rust-analyzer.ts with one regex per field, and the matrix's emit rule is uniform — every one of its 32 emit needles is /\\bfield\\s*:/ — so it can only see a field written as an object-literal key: value. Measured here over both pinned corpora (1481 files, 21527 symbols, 183141 edges), that rule MISREADS 5 of this lane's 32 cells, in BOTH directions. 4 UNDER-claim the field: it is carried by published rows and the needle is blind to HOW it is written (UniversalSymbol.returnType=6825, UniversalSymbol.qualified_calls=11448, UniversalSymbol.ownerName=10769, UniversalSymbol.ownerKind=10769) — returnType, qualified_calls and ownerKind by ASSIGNMENT, ownerName as a SHORTHAND property. 1 OVER-claim one: the needle matches a NESTED metadata.relationshipType literal while no edge carries the top-level field at all (the lane publishes the finer label inside metadata, and the family field \"type\" at the top level). So the non-alive cells P1 lists for rust that sit in the under-claimed set are the PROXY failing, not the lane: qualified_calls is independently pinned by the lane's own fixture (validation/m14/fixtures/rust/qualified-calls-ownership.expectation.json), and returnType/ownerName/ownerKind are carried by thousands of rows at runtime. The needles are left alone on purpose: a literal widening would also match a call ARGUMENT named ownerName, so the honest repair is an AST-based emit probe, which is a M60.P1 instrument change and not this lane's to make. The REMAINING non-alive cells are the disclosure P1 asks for (scope / isStatic / isAsync / isAbstract / decorators / apiContractPath / taintSources / taintSinks / gravityScore / layer / comments / from / to): 0 of 21527 symbols and 0 of 183141 edges carry them, so this lane publishes nothing there — with one worth naming, decorators, where attributes are consumed into frameworkPatterns by the R8 detector and published as neither decorators nor